# GCC spec override for linking real programs against PoC-OS's mlibc
# port with the cross GCC (toolchain/cross/bin/x86_64-elf-gcc, built
# --without-headers, --target=x86_64-elf -- see the top-level Makefile's
# cross-gcc rule). A bare *-elf target's own built-in *startfile/*endfile
# specs assume a freestanding/embedded convention (crt0.o, no crti.o/
# crtn.o split -- confirmed via `x86_64-elf-gcc -dumpspecs`) since it was
# never told about a hosted libc at configure time; our mlibc port
# instead follows the normal hosted convention -- crt1.o's _start calling
# into libc startup, wrapped by crti.o/crtn.o's _init/_fini prologue/
# epilogue (see toolchain/mlibc-sysdeps-pocos/crti.S's doc comment) -- so
# this replaces the two specs that pick which CRT object files get linked
# in, and in what order, with that convention. `%s` means "resolve via
# the same -B/library search path as -l would" -- pass
# -B$(MLIBC_SYSROOT_SHARED)/usr/lib alongside -specs=this-file so it
# resolves to our own crt1.o/crti.o/crtn.o.
#
# GCC's own crtbegin.o/crtend.o (which a hosted target's startfile/endfile
# would normally also include, wrapping the _init/_fini bodies further --
# see crti.S's own doc comment) are deliberately left out: this toolchain
# was configured --disable-shared (see the cross-gcc Makefile target), so
# libgcc only ever built the non-PIC crtbegin.o/crtend.o variant, whose
# __TMC_END__ relocation isn't PIE-safe (verified empirically -- linking
# a -pie binary against it fails with "relocation R_X86_64_32 against
# hidden symbol `__TMC_END__' can not be used when making a PIE object").
# Every consumer here is plain C built with -fno-unwind-tables
# -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables (busybox's own Makefile.flags) with no
# __attribute__((constructor)) globals needing crtbegin/crtend's
# eh-frame/init-array registration, so dropping them doesn't lose
# anything real programs here would exercise.
%rename startfile old_startfile
*startfile:
crt1.o%s crti.o%s

%rename endfile old_endfile
*endfile:
crtn.o%s
